# ===========================================================================
# flags/ffmpeg.flags -- portable configure flags. ONE build serves BOTH
# consumers.
#
# Format: one configure flag per line. `#` starts a comment; blank lines
# ignored. Files are concatenated by scripts/lib/flags.sh in this order, and
# FFmpeg's configure is LAST-WINS for enable/disable, so later files override:
#
#     flags/ffmpeg.flags
#     flags/ffmpeg.<os>.flags
#     flags/ffmpeg.<os>.<arch>.flags      (optional; only linux.arm64 exists)
#
# Every option name AND every component name in these files is checked by
# scripts/validate-flags.sh against the actual pinned FFmpeg source, so a typo
# fails CI instead of silently shipping a build without the feature. That
# check is not theoretical: `--enable-encoder=mov_text` is accepted by
# configure's parser and does nothing, because the configure-time name is
# `movtext`.
#
# PROVENANCE TAGS on each line:
#     # nelux  -- required by the nelux Python wheel (D:\Nelux)
#     # TAS    -- required by TheAnimeScripter and its two front-ends
#     # both   -- required by both
#     # union  -- required by neither today; included under the union rule
#                 because it is free and a consumer could plausibly reach it
#
# UNION RULE (owner's decision): if either consumer needs a feature, it is in.
# Do not trim this file for size. The decisive argument is
# Theanimescripter/src/cli/parser.py:1278-1280 -- `--custom_encoder` is a
# free-text argument with no `choices=`, whitespace-split at
# src/io/ffmpegSettings.py:825 and spliced verbatim into the command line at
# :744, where it REPLACES matchEncoder()'s output entirely. TAS-Standalone
# re-exports it as free text (src/shared/schema.ts:462). The required feature
# set is therefore UNBOUNDED BY CONSTRUCTION and no static analysis can close
# it. See README.md#custom-encoder.
# ===========================================================================

# ===========================================================================
# 1. Licensing
# ===========================================================================
# GPL is a deliberate choice by the owner: libx264 and libx265 are both
# GPL-licensed and both are required. nelux's test suite encodes with them
# (82 and 30 references across Nelux/tests/*.py), and TheAnimeScripter's
# matchEncoder() emits `-c:v libx264` / `-c:v libx265` for 12 of its 37
# presets (Theanimescripter/src/io/encodingSettings.py:10-123).
--enable-gpl                                  # both

# We deliberately do NOT pass --enable-version3.
#
# ffmpeg-8.1.2/configure:2007-2016 defines EXTERNAL_LIBRARY_VERSION3_LIST as
# exactly: gmp, libaribb24, liblensfun, libopencore_amrnb, libopencore_amrwb,
# libvo_amrwbenc, mbedtls, rkmpp. We enable NONE of them, so nothing forces
# version3 on us.
#
# configure:4773 reads:
#     enabled version3 && { enabled gpl && enable gplv3 || enable lgplv3; }
# so --enable-version3 together with --enable-gpl would relicense the whole
# binary as GPLv3. Staying off it keeps the output at "GPL v2 or later" --
# strictly the better position, because it stays combinable with GPLv2-only
# code. x264's COPYING is the plain GPLv2 text and upstream now maintains an
# explicit `GPLv2-only` branch, so this is not hypothetical.
#
# Consequence, enforced in the per-OS files: we must NOT enable OpenSSL >= 3.0.
# configure:7493 hard-fails that combination:
#     { enabled gplv3 || ! enabled gpl || enabled nonfree
#       || die "ERROR: OpenSSL >=3.0.0 requires --enable-version3"; }

# NEVER enable --enable-nonfree: the output would be undistributable, which
# breaks both consumers' distribution model. Stated as an explicit disable so
# an accidental re-enable shows up as a conflict in review.
--disable-nonfree                             # both

# ===========================================================================
# 2. What gets built
# ===========================================================================
# ALL SEVEN LIBRARIES. Under the union rule this is settled by TAS: the ffmpeg
# CLI itself requires avfilter, and TAS uses -vf on nearly every invocation
# (zscale at Theanimescripter/src/io/ffmpegSettings.py:797, hwupload_cuda at
# :737). avdevice comes along for `-f lavfi` / `-f gdigrab` style
# --custom_encoder input.
#
# FOR THE RECORD -- the nelux LIBRARY does not call into avfilter or
# avdevice. Verified: Nelux/include/Nelux/CxCore.hpp:43-45 includes
# avfilter.h/buffersink.h/buffersrc.h and CxCore.hpp:97-103,126 define
# AVFilterGraphDeleter / AVFilterGraphPtr, but a repo-wide grep returns those
# two definitions and ZERO uses -- an unused unique_ptr deleter emits no code.
# av_buffersrc_*, av_buffersink_* and avdevice_*: 0 hits in Nelux/src and
# Nelux/include. Both are still hard-linked (CMakeLists.txt:307 lists all
# seven and :313-319 FATAL_ERRORs on a missing .def; FFmpegDelayLoad.cpp:56
# names them), so building all seven means nelux needs NO CMake change.
#
# avdevice is NOT merely a union courtesy though: nelux's TEST SUITE needs it.
# Nelux/tests/test_codec_container_parity.py:38-42 generates its media with
#     subprocess.run([FFMPEG, ..., "-f", "lavfi",
#                     "-i", "testsrc2=size=...:rate=30", ...], check=True)
# and `lavfi` is an INPUT DEVICE living in libavdevice. check=True means that
# raises rather than skips. Six benchmark scripts do the same.
# scripts/verify-output.sh asserts `device lavfi` after every build.
--enable-avdevice                             # both
--enable-avfilter                             # TAS
--enable-swscale                              # both
--enable-swresample                           # both

# The CLI programs. Hard requirement for TAS:
#   Theanimescripter/src/cli/startup.py:64-86 resolves
#     <root>/ffmpeg_shared/ffmpeg[.exe] and .../ffprobe[.exe]
#     and calls getFFMPEG() if EITHER is missing.
#   src/autoclip/autoclip.py:73-89                    ffprobe -show_entries
#   TAS-Standalone/src/main/tas/probe.ts:152          ffprobe -show_format
#   TAS-AdobeEdition/src/js/main/utils/ffmpegBridge.ts:371  ffprobe likewise
# Against shared libraries these two executables are small (see docs/SIZES.md).
--enable-ffmpeg                               # TAS
--enable-ffprobe                              # TAS
# ffplay is used by nothing and would drag SDL2 into the dependency closure.
--disable-ffplay                              # neither

# SHARED libraries on every platform, and this is dictated by both consumers:
#   * nelux BUNDLES libav*/libsw* inside its wheel and delay-loads them
#     (Nelux/CMakeLists.txt:612-625 emits /DELAYLOAD per av*/sw* DLL).
#   * Theanimescripter/src/infra/getFFMPEG.py:34-42 pins a `*-gpl-shared`
#     archive and :316-335 FLATTENS its `bin/*` into <root>/ffmpeg_shared/,
#     after which src/cli/startup.py:60-79 calls os.add_dll_directory() on
#     that folder *specifically so nelux can delay-load from there*.
# NOTE: on mingw, configure:6142-6145 force-disables static when shared is
# enabled ("Cannot build both shared and static libs when using dllimport"),
# so --disable-static is the honest, explicit spelling of what happens anyway.
--enable-shared                               # both
--disable-static                              # both

# NO --build-suffix. Deliberate reversal of an earlier design.
# Under the one-profile design, nelux's bundled copy and TAS's ffmpeg_shared
# copy are byte-identical builds of the same FFmpeg, so it does not matter
# which one Windows resolves first: same symbols, same ABI, no mismatch.
# That safety holds ONLY while the two stay in lockstep -- see
# README.md#drift and the manifest.json emitted by scripts/make-manifest.sh,
# which exists so each consumer can assert at startup that the FFmpeg it
# loaded is the one it expects.

# ===========================================================================
# 3. Build hygiene
# ===========================================================================
--disable-debug                               # both -- no -g; neither consumer debugs into libav*
--disable-doc                                 # both -- man/html/texi not shipped
--disable-htmlpages                           # both
--disable-manpages                            # both
--disable-podpages                            # both
--disable-txtpages                            # both

# ===========================================================================
# 4. Determinism -- the most important flag in this file
# ===========================================================================
# Do NOT let configure pick up whatever happens to be installed on the build
# machine. Every external library must be requested explicitly below, so an
# unpinned system libmp3lame / libass / libvorbis can never silently enter the
# binary and change its size, its ABI surface, or our licence obligations.
--disable-autodetect                          # both

# *** TRAP -- READ BEFORE EDITING ***
# --disable-autodetect turns off AUTODETECT_LIBS, which ffmpeg-8.1.2/configure
# defines as
#     EXTERNAL_AUTODETECT_LIBRARY_LIST + HWACCEL_AUTODETECT_LIBRARY_LIST
#     + THREADS_LIST
# THREADS_LIST is "pthreads os2threads w32threads", so --disable-autodetect
# silently produces a SINGLE-THREADED FFmpeg unless threading is re-enabled by
# name. That is done in flags/ffmpeg.<os>.flags. Likewise zlib,
# mediafoundation, schannel, securetransport, videotoolbox, amf, cuda, cuvid,
# nvenc, nvdec, ffnvcodec, vaapi, d3d11va and dxva2 are ALL in those lists and
# are ALL off until explicitly named.

# zlib is mandatory, not optional: png/apng encode+decode, ffv1, flashsv, the
# matroska/mov compressed-header paths and several image decoders need it.
# Built from source as a static library (versions.lock ZLIB_*) so the produced
# libav* DLLs never acquire a runtime dependency on MSYS2's zlib1.dll -- which
# would be an ImportError on every nelux user's machine, since the wheel ships
# only av*/sw*.
--enable-zlib                                 # both

# Matroska supports zlib/bzlib/lzma header stripping and track compression;
# TIFF and several image decoders use lzma. Both consumers ingest arbitrary
# user media.
--enable-bzlib                                # both
--enable-lzma                                 # both
# Charset conversion for subtitle demuxers and non-UTF-8 metadata tags. TAS
# transcodes subtitles to webvtt/mov_text (ffmpegSettings.py:860) and nelux
# does subtitle passthrough (Nelux/src/Nelux/backends/Encoder.cpp:525-527).
--enable-iconv                                # both

# Runtime CPU dispatch stays ON. We must never bake in -march=native or an ISA
# level: one artefact has to run on every x86-64 machine.
--enable-runtime-cpudetect                    # both
# PIC everywhere: required for ELF shared objects, and required because the
# libraries get loaded into a Python process.
--enable-pic                                  # both

# NOTE: --enable-lto is deliberately NOT set. It is a known source of
# miscompiles in FFmpeg's hand-written-asm interaction and of very long link
# times against the x265 static blob, and it has never been measured here.

# ===========================================================================
# 5. Components -- everything default-on stays on
# ===========================================================================
# There is deliberately NO --disable-encoders / --disable-decoders /
# --disable-muxers / --disable-demuxers / --disable-filters / --disable-bsfs
# in this file. Under the union rule, and given the unbounded --custom_encoder
# surface, trimming buys a few MB and costs unpredictable user-visible
# "Unknown encoder" failures.
#
# The closed sets that static analysis DID prove are required are listed
# below purely as documentation, and are asserted after every build by
# scripts/verify-output.sh against the generated config.h.
#
# --- nelux ---------------------------------------------------------------
#   DEFAULT ENCODER  the single hardcoded name "h264_mf", with no probe and no
#                    platform guard (Nelux/src/Nelux/python/VideoEncoder.cpp:58
#                    `props.codec = codec.value_or("h264_mf")`; the docstring at
#                    python/Bindings.cpp:544 says the same).
#                    RE-VERIFIED against nelux b4a57fd. An EARLIER nelux
#                    revision probed h264_mf -> libx264 -> libopenh264 on
#                    Windows and libx264 -> h264_videotoolbox -> libopenh264
#                    elsewhere; that probe is GONE -- `libopenh264` and
#                    `videotoolbox` now have zero hits in all of Nelux/src and
#                    Nelux/include. Anything below that cites the probe is
#                    describing history, not current code.
#                    Consequence for THIS build: --enable-mediafoundation is
#                    load-bearing on Windows, because h264_mf is not a first
#                    preference any more, it is the only default there is.
#   NVENC names hardcoded at Nelux/include/Nelux/backends/Encoder.hpp:22-24:
#                    h264_nvenc, hevc_nvenc, av1_nvenc
#   AV1 decoder preference chain, Nelux/src/Nelux/backends/Decoder.cpp:151:
#                    {"libdav1d", "libaom-av1", "av1"}
#   CUVID table, Nelux/src/Nelux/backends/cuda/Decoder.cpp:509-533 (all nine):
#                    h264_cuvid hevc_cuvid vp8_cuvid vp9_cuvid av1_cuvid
#                    mpeg1_cuvid mpeg2_cuvid mpeg4_cuvid vc1_cuvid
#   MUXERS -- the complete set nelux can emit, from inferContainerFormat()
#             (Nelux/src/Nelux/backends/Encoder.cpp:1509-1524):
#                    mp4 matroska mov webm avi  (everything else falls back to mp4)
#   AUDIO transcode targets, Encoder.cpp:398-400:  AAC OPUS VORBIS MP3
#   SUBTITLE transcode targets, Encoder.cpp:525-527: mov_text webvtt ass subrip
#   TEST SUITE exercises: libx264 libx264rgb rawvideo h264_nvenc libx265 png
#                    libsvtav1 hevc_nvenc libaom-av1 ffv1 h264_qsv libvpx-vp9
#                    libvpx(vp8) hevc_qsv av1_qsv vp9_qsv av1_nvenc prores
#                    prores_ks prores_aw mpeg4 mjpeg huffyuv ffvhuff utvideo
#                    dnxhd h264_mf hevc_mf h264_amf hevc_amf
#   PROTOCOLS: `file` alone for the LIBRARY. Zero URL-scheme literals in
#                    Nelux/src or Nelux/include; avio_open (Encoder.cpp:249)
#                    and avformat_open_input (Encoder.cpp:292, Decoder.cpp:
#                    416/502/2148) always take a plain local path, and
#                    Encoder.cpp:229-239 create the parent directory and
#                    pre-touch the file with std::ofstream, so a URL could
#                    not reach FFmpeg even if one were passed.
#                    The bundled ffmpeg.exe that nelux's tests shell out to
#                    additionally needs `pipe` (tests/test_batch_color_matrix
#                    .py:46 "pipe:1"; tests/test_set_range_identity.py:62 and
#                    tests/test_set_ranges_segments.py:65 "pipe:0").
#   CLI FEATURES used by nelux's own tests through the bundled ffmpeg:
#                    the `lavfi` INDEV (`-f lavfi`) with the testsrc /
#                    testsrc2 / mandelbrot sources, and the scale, zscale,
#                    format, null, psnr, ssim and libvmaf filters
#                    (tests/test_codec_container_parity.py:40-41,
#                    tests/test_software_encoders.py:181,
#                    tests/benchmark_quality.py:26-28). `lavfi` is an indev,
#                    so it needs --enable-avdevice -- which is why avdevice
#                    is not merely a union courtesy.
#   MUXERS/DEMUXERS beyond the five nelux writes, exercised through the CLI
#                    and through tests/data/metadata_corpus: nut,
#                    yuv4mpegpipe (.y4m), mpegts (.ts), mxf, ogg, flv, asf,
#                    wav, image2. All default-on; listed so verify-output.sh
#                    can assert them.
#   AUDIO/SUBTITLE decoders needed by the corpus: aac, mp3, flac, opus,
#                    vorbis, subrip, movtext, ass.
#   Nelux/README.md:237 promises "anything libavcodec can decode", and :243
#   "anything libavformat can demux" -- which is why no decoder or demuxer is
#   ever disabled in this file.
#
# --- TAS ------------------------------------------------------------------
#   ENCODERS -- all 15 `-c:v` values emitted by matchEncoder()
#             (Theanimescripter/src/io/encodingSettings.py:10-307):
#                    libx264 libx265 h264_nvenc hevc_nvenc av1_nvenc h264_qsv
#                    hevc_qsv vp9_qsv libsvtav1 h264_amf hevc_amf prores_ks
#                    gif libvpx-vp9 png
#             plus  aac, libopus              (ffmpegSettings.py:846-858)
#                   mov_text, webvtt          (ffmpegSettings.py:848-860)
#                   mjpeg                     (TAS-Standalone probe.ts:209,
#                                              TAS-AdobeEdition
#                                              autoCutReviewOrchestrator.ts:344,
#                                              MediaBrowserTab.tsx:794)
#                   rawvideo                  (the `-f null -` sink)
#   MUXERS   mp4 (incl. fragmented -movflags frag_keyframe+empty_moov+
#            default_base_moof and +faststart), mov, matroska, webm, avi, gif,
#            image2 (PNG sequences, ffmpegSettings.py:492-502), mjpeg, null,
#            ipod (.m4a audio-only), plus flv, mpegts, ogg from
#            TAS-AdobeEdition's container map (ffmpegBridge.ts:259-272)
#   DEMUXERS rawvideo (ffmpegSettings.py:627-636, :681-699 -- the ONLY
#            explicit -f on an input), image2, mov/mp4, matroska/webm,
#            avi, gif, mjpeg, image_png_pipe, flv, mpegts, ogg,
#            plus hls (see PROTOCOLS)
#   FILTERS  scale (with flags=bilinear, flags=lanczos, in_range/out_range/
#            out_color_matrix, and expression eval -- probe.ts:213 uses
#            scale='min(1280,iw)':-2), format (gray, gray16be, yuva420p,
#            yuv444p16le, nv12, p010le), setparams, zscale, hwupload_cuda,
#            null, anull
#   PROTOCOLS file, pipe (pipe:0 / pipe:1 / `-`), and -- reachable, not
#            hypothetical -- https + tls + crypto + the hls demuxer.
#            Theanimescripter/src/ytdlp.py:184 and :202 set
#                "ffmpeg_location": os.path.dirname(FFMPEGPATH)
#            which points yt-dlp at OUR ffmpeg. TAS sets neither
#            --downloader nor hls_prefer_native (0 hits in all three repos),
#            so yt-dlp's own defaults apply: it falls back to FFmpegFD --
#            spawning our ffmpeg with an https://...m3u8 URL -- for live and
#            live-from-start YouTube formats, which processUrlInput
#            (src/io/inputNormalization.py:19-22) happily accepts.
#            This is what makes a TLS backend a requirement rather than a
#            courtesy; see flags/ffmpeg.linux.flags.
#   BSFs     none named explicitly; aac_adtstoasc and h264_mp4toannexb are
#            auto-inserted by the `-c copy` remux paths in TAS-AdobeEdition
#            (ffmpegBridge.ts:361-421) and by yt-dlp's merge step
#            (Theanimescripter/src/ytdlp.py:184-206)
#
# NOT needed by any consumer, and NOT enabled anywhere -- each would add
# another pinned dependency and they are collectively most of the difference
# between our avcodec and BtbN's 98.9 MB one: libass / libfreetype /
# libfontconfig / libharfbuzz (no subtitles= / ass / drawtext filter anywhere
# in any of the three repos), libbluray, libsrt, librist, libzmq,
# libmp3lame, libtwolame, libtheora, libvorbis, libspeex, libgsm, libilbc,
# libsoxr, libssh, libopenmpt, libmodplug, libgme, libshine, libsnappy,
# libxml2, libplacebo, vulkan, chromaprint, frei0r, libvidstab,
# librubberband, avisynth, libjxl, libwebp, libopenjpeg, librav1e, libxvid.
#
# librav1e / libxvid / libtheora / libwebp / libjxl / libopenjpeg deserve a
# note because they DO appear in Nelux/tests/output/gray_all_codecs.py:44-65
# and :116-122. That script is not a requirement list: its docstring (:1-8)
# says "For each encoder that FFmpeg reports", and EXT_BY_NAME is a
# container-preference lookup table for encoders that may or may not exist.
# Checked before excluding them.

# ===========================================================================
# 6. External libraries
# ===========================================================================
--enable-libx264                              # both -- nelux tests (82 refs); TAS 12 presets
--enable-libx265                              # both -- nelux tests (30 refs); TAS presets x265*
--enable-libsvtav1                            # both -- nelux special-cases it at Encoder.cpp:1193,:1276,:1497; TAS av1/slow_av1
--enable-libdav1d                             # nelux -- FIRST in the AV1 decoder chain, Decoder.cpp:151
--enable-libaom                                # both -- SECOND in nelux's AV1 chain; also the libaom-av1 encoder
--enable-libvpx                               # both -- TAS `-c:v libvpx-vp9` (encodingSettings.py:268); nelux tests libvpx-vp9
--enable-libopus                              # both -- TAS muxes Opus into .webm (ffmpegSettings.py:848); nelux audio transcode target
# libzimg backs the `zscale` filter. Theanimescripter/src/io/ffmpegSettings.py
# :797 emits `zscale=matrix=bt2020:norm=bt2020:dither=error_diffusion` for the
# BT.2020 path. Without libzimg that filter does not exist and every BT.2020
# render fails. (The BT.709 path was deliberately moved off zscale onto
# swscale for ~3x CPU -- see the comment at ffmpegSettings.py:776-790 -- but
# the BT.2020 path is still reachable.)
--enable-libzimg                              # TAS

# libopenh264 -- KEPT ON THE UNION RULE ONLY. Its original justification is
# gone: it was the last-resort entry of nelux's default-encoder probe, and
# that probe no longer exists (see the DEFAULT ENCODER note above). As of
# nelux b4a57fd there are ZERO references to openh264 in any of the four
# consumer repos.
#
# It stays because it is a plausible `--custom_encoder` / `codec=` string for
# an H.264 encoder, and because the file's standing rule is over-inclusion.
# It is, however, the one dependency here that nothing measurable needs: drop
# --enable-libopenh264, its OPENH264_* pin in versions.lock, build_openh264 in
# scripts/build-deps.sh and the `encoder libopenh264` line in
# flags/required-components.txt together, and the build loses a pinned source,
# a compile step and ~1 MB. BSD-2-Clause, so no licence interaction with
# --enable-gpl.
# NOTE: we build OpenH264 from source, so Cisco's H.264 royalty coverage --
# which applies only to Cisco's own prebuilt binaries -- does NOT apply.
# That is the same patent position we already have from libx264/libx265.
--enable-libopenh264                          # nelux

# libvmaf backs the `libvmaf` FILTER, and this is a hard test requirement,
# not a nice-to-have. Nelux/tests/test_software_encoders.py is a real pytest
# module (parametrised test_software_encoder_pixfmt at :226) whose quality
# gate at :181 runs
#     [0:v][1:v]libvmaf=feature='name=psnr|name=float_ssim'
# through the bundled ffmpeg and RAISES RuntimeError at :198 when that fails.
# It skips only when ffmpeg/ffprobe are absent (:171, :221) -- a missing
# libvmaf filter is a test FAILURE, not a skip. Seven more benchmark scripts
# use it (bench_encode_quality.py:143, bench_full_matrix.py:229,
# bench_scaler_matrix.py:181, comprehensive_bench.py:294,
# pixfmt_matrix_bench.py:243, bench_workflow.py:217).
# BSD-2-Clause-Patent; models are compiled in (no runtime .json files).
# `float_ssim` needs libvmaf's float feature extractors -- build-deps.sh
# passes -Denable_float=true explicitly rather than relying on the default.
--enable-libvmaf                              # nelux

# MP3 ENCODING: deliberately NOT provided, and this is now a decision rather
# than a gap. Both claims were checked against the consumers' source:
#   * nelux -- AV_CODEC_ID_MP3 at Encoder.cpp:399 is the 4th entry of a
#     PROBE list (Encoder.cpp:398-400) inside setupAudioTranscode. Every
#     candidate is gated on `avformat_query_codec(...) && avcodec_find_
#     encoder(c)` at :404-405, native AV_CODEC_ID_AAC is first, and the whole
#     list is only consulted when av_guess_codec() came back empty
#     (Encoder.cpp:396). For the five containers inferContainerFormat() can
#     produce (Encoder.cpp:1509-1524) av_guess_codec never comes back empty,
#     and AAC/Opus/Vorbis encoders are all present regardless. The MP3 entry
#     degrades cleanly when no MP3 encoder exists.
#   * TAS -- zero references to mp3/libmp3lame/wav/flac/m4a anywhere under
#     Theanimescripter/src. The only `-c:a` line in the whole repo is
#     ffmpegSettings.py:860, fed by :846-859 with exactly
#     {copy, libopus, aac}.
# MP3 *decoding* works (native decoder, always on) -- which is what the
# metadata corpus (Nelux/tests/data/metadata_corpus/D/*mp3*.mp4) exercises.
# If a --custom_encoder user ever needs `-c:a libmp3lame`, pin LAME 3.100
# (SHA256 ddfe36cab873794038ae2c1210557ad34857a4b6bdc515785d1da9e175b1da1e,
# verified 2026-08-04) and add one line here.
